From cd81bbfcb13ab984e0016fe5c0e62d568c98e035 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 19:59:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/12] T1: a suite that looks at the application
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`layout.mjs` asks whether the geometry is sound. It is a good suite, and the
first time anybody ran this application at a size they could see it had nine
faults in it and `layout.mjs` had reported none of them — because not one was a
geometry fault. A tracklist squeezed to four pixels fits its box. A tonearm
drawn at a fifteenth of its length overlaps nothing. Two spans that set on one
line when they were meant to stack are, geometrically, in perfect order.

So `looks.mjs` asks the other question, in two halves.

A LINT of five rules, each one the shape of a defect that actually shipped: a
flex child squeezed below the height its own content needs; `overflow: hidden`
cutting content where no mask says it should; text under seven effective
pixels, measured across the cross axis as well, which is how a vertical spine
four pixels wide is caught; two text boxes on top of each other; an interactive
control under the transport.

Three things separate it from a rule that cries wolf, and each cost a round to
learn. A rectangle is where an element *would* be — inside a virtualised list
that is hundreds of pixels past the fold, so every rect is intersected with its
clipping ancestors first, or every list in the app reports controls buried
under the transport. `text-overflow: ellipsis` is a promise the cut is
deliberate, but only along the inline axis, which a vertical writing mode turns
on its side. And a box that overflows must have a *visible* child doing it: the
immersive stage hides its own chrome by sliding it ten pixels down at zero
opacity, and reported ten pixels of overflow for a whole run before that rule
was right. `data-clips` is an element saying the cut is the point — the crate
carries it, because a crate that fits inside its own box is a shelf.

And GOLDENS: 57 surfaces — every route, all four album views, the album page
and its back, the queue, the stage, the turntable, the shortcut overlay — in
dark and light at 1440 and again at 620, compared pixel for pixel with the last
accepted run. Changing one design token is caught on nineteen of them.

A golden is only worth having if it is stable, and three separate things in
here move on their own. Canvases are hidden and repeating animations parked.
Entrances are *finished*, not paused: paused at zero photographs the state a
page is leaving, which is a state nobody ever looks at, and it was reporting a
heading still sitting where it had begun as ten pixels of overflow. Playback is
loaded and then stopped, because a run that keeps playing lights a different row
of the songs list in every sweep. And the tonearm is pinned, because it *is* the
playhead and is therefore never twice the same — the golden checks how the arm
is drawn, and where it ought to be at a given moment is arithmetic, checked
elsewhere. Three consecutive runs are identical; a one-token colour change
fails nineteen surfaces.

PNG comes in by hand, since Node has no decoder: 8-bit non-interlaced, RGB or
RGBA — Chromium picks per shot depending on whether the page is opaque, and
decoding only one of them is a crash halfway through the second run.

Four faults it found on its first pass, all at 620px, all fixed here:

- The collapsed sidebar's Add button kept `padding: 0 18px` around a 15px icon
  in a 40px box, because `.btn` in components.css sets the same property at the
  same specificity and loads later. Two classes deep, and it means what it says.
- The album hero's four controls and three gaps come to exactly the width of a
  620px hero, and `nowrap` spent the shortfall on the only button with a label:
  Shuffle lost fifteen pixels of its own name. They wrap now, as the home hero
  already did.
- The metadata line wrapped between a fact and the separator belonging to it,
  leaving "AMBROSE VALE · 2020 ·" hanging at the end of a line. Year, tracks and
  runtime travel as one unit now, so the only break is after the artist, and the
  separator that joined them is hidden where the wrap happens.
- The back cover's guarantees were written in absolute pixels for a 232px
  sleeve, and needed 213px of the 168px square it gets on a narrow screen.
  Everything gives a little there: two tracks and three rows of fine print, and
  the rest runs out under the fades it already has.

Verified: looks clean at 57 surfaces over three runs; layout clean at every
width; smoke and interactions unchanged and green.

