From 78a5cfb6a239b0429d6889583ba9ee160fff77f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:15:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/12] A5, D1 and D2: one keyboard table, and a library the
 browser has agreed to keep
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A5 — THE KEYBOARD

There were two lists: a `switch` on a keystroke that did the work, and a
hand-written array that drew the `?` overlay. The README was honest about it —
"a second list rather than a projection of the key handler" — and honest about
why it stayed that way, that making them one meant rewriting the handler to be
data-driven and that this was a bigger change than it sounds. In the meantime
they drifted again: ⌘Y ran redo and appeared in neither the overlay nor the
README's own table.

`js/keys.js` is the table. A binding is an id, a combo, a group, a label and
something to run; `bind()` registers one, the handler dispatches from it, the
overlay renders from it. A shortcut not in the table does not exist, and one in
it cannot go undocumented — ⌘Y is now printed, under Redo, as the second
spelling it is.

Four things the rewrite had to keep, all of which were load-bearing:

- "?" is registered before "/" because Shift is held to type it on most layouts
  and it arrives as Shift+"/" on the rest, which is the same physical key as
  search. Order is priority, so this is expressed by where it sits.
- Redo is registered before Undo, or ⌘⇧Z is swallowed by the ⌘Z that matches
  it first.
- Neither undo binding fires while a text field has the caret: inside the edit
  dialog ⌘Z means "undo what I just typed", which is the browser's job.
- A binding with no modifiers must not match when one is held, which is what the
  old `if (typing || metaKey || ctrlKey || altKey) return` gate did in one line
  and the combo parser now does per binding.

The immersive view registers its own two, at module load rather than on open,
with an `active` predicate. That is what lets L and D be inert while the stage
is shut and still appear in the overlay — the list should be the whole list, not
the list of what happens to be on screen. Group order is declared rather than
inherited from import order, which otherwise put the visualiser above Playback.

`Mod` is ⌘ on a Mac and Ctrl everywhere else. Overrides are stored per id and
resolved over the defaults, so remapping is editing the table — which is the
half of A4 that belongs here rather than in its own change.

D1 — STORAGE THAT SURVIVES

Nothing called `navigator.storage.persist()`, and everything this application
lets you change is an overlay in IndexedDB: playlists, favourites, tag
corrections, the covers you chose, racks bound to records, every hour of
listening it has counted. Without that grant it is *best-effort* storage, which
a browser short of room may evict without asking — and there is no server copy
to come back from, because there is no server.

It asks once there is something to lose, straight after the first import, rather
than at boot: an empty index is nothing to protect and Firefox would be
prompting before the app had shown what it is for. Settings reports what came
back rather than assuming, and offers to ask again — Chromium decides from how
the site is used rather than by prompting, so a refusal is a "not yet" and the
row says so in those words.

D2 — AND SAYS WHEN IT IS RUNNING OUT

`db.usage()` already had the estimate; nothing did anything with the ratio. Past
four fifths of the browser's allowance the row says so in the warn colour,
because running out is quiet: a new import half-lands, a playlist does not save,
and nothing about that failure names the cause.

Found while wiring it: `db` was not imported in app.js at all. `node --check`
validates syntax and not module resolution, which is a good argument for the
suite below being the thing that says whether a change works.

Verified: smoke, interactions, audio and layout all green; looks clean at 57
surfaces — and it caught the extra overlay row this change adds, which is what
it is for.

