From 91a5af32620b10a0b4c74665703368c3ecd4ea97 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 21:05:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/12] L1, L2, L3, L5, L6 and L16: ordering a wall, and
 finding a row in it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Six library changes, and two framework faults found by building them.

L1 — SORTING ALBUMS AND ARTISTS

Songs has had sortable column headers since the first release, because a table
has somewhere to put them. Albums had four *view modes* and not one sort, and
Artists had neither — so a wall of records was always in whatever order the
index happened to hold it, and there was no way to ask for 1978, or the longest
record, or the one nobody has played.

A menu rather than a row of segmented buttons: eight keys in a segmented
control is a wall of words competing with the four view modes beside it, where
a menu is one button that says what the order currently is. Choosing the key
that is already on turns it round, which is what a column header does and the
thing people try first. Remembered per page. Hidden on the Floor, which orders
itself by release year — that is the whole of what it is, and offering a
control there that does nothing is worse than not offering one.

L2 — PLAY COUNT AND LAST PLAYED

Both counted since the first release, read by the smart shelves, and displayed
nowhere: the application has been keeping a record of your listening and not
showing it to you. Two columns in Songs, two sorts, and two more figures rolled
up onto albums and artists during the reindex that already walks every track.

A never-played row prints a dash and not a nought — "0" invites comparison with
the numbers above it and there is nothing to compare; the point of the column is
which rows are blank. `fmtAgo` writes the date as an age, because twenty rows of
"14/03/2025" is not something anybody can scan; the exact date is in the title.

L3 — RECENTLY PLAYED

`history.recent` — sixty ids, newest first, written on every play and restored
from IndexedDB — has existed since the first release and the only thing that
ever read it was one button on Home. There was no page to go to, which makes
"what was that?" a question this could not answer. Newest first and deliberately
not sortable: it is a log, and the order is the information.

L5 and L6 — GETTING TO A ROW

A letter rail down the right edge, and typing. Letters the collection has
nothing under stay drawn and go quiet: a rail that changes shape as a library
grows is one nobody's hand can learn, and the gaps are information — a
collection with no Q in it looks like one. Typing accumulates for three quarters
of a second so "bea" finds Beacon rather than stopping at the first B, and it
only takes over on the *second* character, because the single-letter transport
shortcuts live on the same keys: "n" is next track, "no" is looking for
Nocturne. Both match on whichever column the list is ordered by; jumping
alphabetically through a list sorted by length is not a feature.

The rail hangs on `.main` rather than inside `#view`, because a navigation aid
appended to the scroller scrolls away with the thing it steers.

L16 — TWO MORE SHELVES THE LIBRARY WRITES

"Never played", oldest first, so the records that have been waiting longest come
up — on a large collection that is the most useful question there is, and
nothing had ever asked it. And "You used to play these": five plays or more and
untouched for a year, which reads as a suggestion rather than an accusation. A
record with two plays a year ago is one you tried; one with twenty is one you
loved and forgot.

TWO FAULTS FOUND WHILE BUILDING THEM

`VirtualGrid.setItems` did not re-render. It called `measure()`, which only
rebuilds when the *geometry* changes — and reordering a list of the same length
at the same width changes neither the column count nor the row height, so the
cells kept their old contents and a re-sorted grid showed the old order. The
list next door has always recycled on new data; the grid had only ever been
handed a different *count*, so nothing had noticed. Sorting Artists is the first
thing that asked it to.

`scrollToIndex` landed short, twice over. It computed `i * rowHeight` as an
absolute scroll position while `update()` had always subtracted the sizer's
offset within the scroller — so a jump missed by however much page sits above
the list, about four rows on Songs. And it took no account of a sticky column
header, so the row it did reach arrived forty pixels underneath the thing naming
its columns: scrolled to, and invisible. A sticky element pins at the
scrollport's padding edge plus its own offset, so that is what is measured —
rather than assumed, because the album page has no header and the queue has a
different one.

Verified: smoke, interactions, audio and layout green; looks clean at 57
surfaces over two runs, with the lint reporting nothing on any of the new
surfaces.

