From 0c201c3e88af0503bf1a3679bb13540c15f8528c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 00:58:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/12] Playback, the queue, and the room it is played in
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Q1, Q3, Q4, Q7–Q10, R1–R5, R12, R13, R15, A2, R3, plus two faults the
work uncovered.

The queue
  The played half is folded away behind a count and can be shown again;
  rows carry their queue index rather than their position in the visible
  list, because folding makes those two different numbers and acting on
  the wrong one removes the wrong track. Rows take focus: Enter plays,
  Delete removes and moves focus to what took its place, Alt+Up/Down
  reorders and says so to a screen reader. Where a track came from is
  printed beside the artist, and only where it differs from what the
  queue as a whole says.

Playback
  The crossfade has a shape now — equal power or straight lines — and
  the row appears with the slider rather than sitting greyed out. Output
  can be routed to a device other than the system default, asked for
  only when wanted, because naming the outputs costs a microphone grant
  everywhere but Firefox. Position, duration and rate are pushed to the
  OS media controls, throttled. A recording over twenty minutes
  remembers where it was left, resumes there, says so, and offers to
  start over; the mark is written on pause as well as on pagehide,
  because a write started as the page is torn down is not guaranteed to
  land.

The room
  The Floor knows what is playing and walks to it on P, with a lamp on
  that record. A decade rail down the right edge says which decade you
  are in and takes you to another. Enter steps into the room and the
  arrows then move between records rather than walking the camera, with
  the camera following so the focused record is never behind you. The
  run starts at a gutter instead of the horizontal centre, so the left
  forty per cent of the room is no longer empty floor.

  The Crate says where you are in it, and the records you have already
  passed lean the other way instead of standing in the same fan as the
  ones still to come. F turns the front record over.

  The Shelf grows dividers between groups, keyed to whatever it is
  sorted by: initials for artist or title, decades for year, and none
  for a sort no divider could label.

  The back cover gets the 272px it needs to print all four of its
  blocks, and its spec sets as two pairs to a line above 900px. The
  transport's spectrum ribbon comes inside the bar's own edge at 10px,
  which is the hairline the README always described.

Two faults found on the way
  Recycled rows came back from the pool last-in-first-out, so a data
  change left a whole screen of them in exactly reverse DOM order.
  Nothing looked wrong — rows are placed by transform — but Tab, screen
  readers and anything else that walks the document read that order, and
  the queue's new keyboard reordering would have tabbed backwards. Both
  virtualisers now put their live rows back in index order, checked with
  a walk and repaired only when wrong.

  The shelf's dividers were bucketing every record under "A" because
  formatName() is a file-format helper, not a name one. The letter rule
  the A–Z rail already had is now shared, so the two agree.

Also: looks.mjs took --accept as its golden directory, blessed every
surface into a folder called "--accept" and reported a clean run. Flags
are stripped before the positional arguments are read, and settings
gained a second surface below the fold, which is where most of it lives.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
