From a41032062b5352e1bacc79b617fc61d1ee982a3a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 05:33:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/12] A copy that lives somewhere else, and a stack you can
 see
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

D3, D4, D5.

Sonora has no account and no server: the library is a folder on your
disk, and everything the application knows about it lives in one
browser's IndexedDB. That is the design, and it had a corollary nobody
had drawn — there was no other copy of months of corrections anywhere,
and "Clear library" was honest about destroying them with no way to take
a copy first.

A backup is one JSON file: playlists, favourites, corrections with what
each one replaced, chosen covers, saved racks and bindings, listening
totals, the Look, and the settings. Not the audio, and not the artwork
thumbnails unless asked — they are the only large thing here and they
rebuild from your files in seconds. Not the folder handles either: a
File System Access handle means nothing outside the profile that granted
it, so a restore names the folders it expects and asks you to point at
them.

It comes back merged, never replaced, and previewed first — the common
case is a new browser on the same machine where the tracks have just
been rescanned and only the overlays are missing, so replacing would
throw away everything done since. The preview says how many of the
corrections match tracks this library actually has, which is the
difference between a restore and a hope. Settings are off by default and
asked for separately: what you want back is the work, not another
machine's crossfade.

The undo stack is visible. It reaches every change the index holds and
its entire interface was ⌘Z, so you could not see what you had done,
could not tell what a run of edits amounted to, and could not step back
past one thing to reach another. Newest at the top, which is the order
they undo in; what has already been taken back stays listed and greyed,
because an entry you cannot see is one you will not think to put back.
The button says "back to here", not "undo this one" — the stack is
ordered and a hole in it is not a thing that can exist.

Two faults found building it
  A restore landed one undo entry per playlist and per favourite as well
  as one for the restore, because every mutator records its own. Undo
  gained `silence()` — the same flag the inverses already use — so a
  caller that will record one entry covering the whole of what it did
  can stop the parts recording themselves.

  Restoring a snapshot could not remove a field. `Object.assign` writes
  what the row has and leaves what it does not, so putting back a track
  from before a correction left the correction's `edits` in place and
  `applyEdits` promptly put the corrected title back. A restore that
  cannot clear a field is not a restore.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JjdT29yrXTcWsnnPfvxBD9
