From 10fa2f19cb25e8072f055a24347a8cc9809428a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 05:59:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/12] Genres, compilations, and one page for everything that
 needs a human
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

L4, L8, L9, L11, R8. L10 turned out to be already built.

Genre is parsed out of every container the tag reader handles and there
was no genre route, no genre on an album page, and no way to say
"everything ambient" except typing it into search and hoping. There is a
wall of them now, weighted by how much of the library each holds — by
the fourth root of the share, so a library that is nine tenths one genre
is a ranking rather than one enormous tile and forty slivers. Genre in
the wild is free text and often a list, so it splits on the three
separators everybody uses and keeps the best-looking spelling for
display.

Compilations. The merge logic is careful and right — a guessed artist
counts as no artist, two records that both name themselves never merge —
but a Various Artists compilation is the one case where the album artist
and the track artists are *supposed* to disagree, and nothing in the
model said so. A twenty-track compilation with no ALBUMARTIST frame did
not merely read as twenty artists: it read as twenty *albums* of one
track each, because the album key is a hash of album artist plus title.

The evidence the merge does not use is the folder. Files in one
directory under one album title are one album whatever their artist tags
say, and that is not what two different records sharing a title look
like. So compilations fold by title and directory, then are marked
either by what they say ("Various Artists" is believed) or by their
shape — several artists, no consistent album artist, and enough of them
that this is the record rather than one guest verse on it. Requiring the
distinct artists to cover two fifths of the tracks is what keeps a record
with two features from being filed under Various. Verified both ways: an
eight-track compilation collapses from nine artists to two, and the
ordinary test library is untouched at eight artists and thirteen albums.

Needs attention gathers six findings the application already had and
kept in six different places — untagged files, missing covers, probable
duplicates, files this browser cannot decode, suspected transcodes, and
partly-guessed tags. One walk computes all of them. Each is a count, the
list behind it, and one thing to do about it.

Find and replace across a field, previewed before it writes anything.
Text rather than a regular expression: a find-and-replace that accepts
`.*` is one that can empty a library by accident, and nobody typing
"feat." wants a character class. Case-insensitive matching leaves the
surrounding text's own case alone. The whole run is one undo.

A multi-disc release already drew as more than one sleeve and already
had disc headings in the page's tracklist, and then the back cover
flattened it into one numbered run where track 1 appeared twice. It
prints the sides now, and the spec block counts them.

