From b60e4daf042e712f835b478b7fca6a7abfc43c2f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 06:15:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/12] Playlists as files, folders for them, cue sheets, and
 two indexes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

L12, L13, L14, L15, L17, L18.

Playlists leave the building. Sonora's whole premise is that the library
is a folder on your disk and nothing is locked away — and then playlists,
the one thing you actually author here, existed only inside IndexedDB,
reachable by nothing else and lost with a Clear library. That is the same
objection this application makes to every other player, turned inward.
They export as Extended M3U with paths relative to the music folder, and
read back the same way.

Matching on the way in is by the tail of the path, longest first: a path
from another player is a path from another machine, and the part two
spellings of the same file share is the end. A bare filename is accepted
only when it names exactly one track, because every rip has an
"01 Intro.mp3". Whatever does not match is listed rather than dropped —
"6 of 60 not found, here they are" is a usable answer and a silent
54-track playlist is not.

The .m3u files already in the folder are noticed during the scan and
offered at the end of an import. Offered, not imported: a music folder
can also hold another player's auto-generated "Recently Added.m3u".

Folders for playlists, one level deep — two levels is a file manager and
nobody has wanted one for forty playlists — and drag to reorder, both
persisted. Removing a folder does not remove what is in it: a folder
here is a label, not a place.

Cue sheets. A large part of the world's carefully ripped music is one
FLAC per side with a .cue beside it — live sets, DJ mixes, anything from
before the CD — and Sonora saw one forty-five-minute track with no
title. A sheet is now parsed into pieces over the one decoded file: each
gets a stable id, its own title and artist, its own length, and its own
place in the album. The side itself stays in the index because it is
what actually gets decoded, and is hidden from the library because a
side listed beside its own eleven tracks is the same record twice.

The playback layer needed three things for that, and each is small:
`startOf` returns the index rather than the lead-in trim; the position
readout and the scrubber work in the piece's time while the element
works in the file's; and a piece ends on the clock at the next index
rather than waiting for an `ended` that never comes in the middle of a
file. `loadedmetadata` no longer overwrites a piece's length with the
file's — verified on a sixty-second file cut into four fifteen-second
pieces, which report 0:15, seek within themselves, and hand over at the
right instant.

Two indexes. Chosen covers and bound racks are listed in Settings with a
way back to each — an override you cannot find is one you cannot undo six
months later. And a library imported before Sonora recorded which fields
it had guessed can have those marks backfilled, through the same parser
the import uses, in batches that are written before the next is asked
for; the row only appears when there is something to do.

