From ddfc0e1e64b3a62ae5917567622d6bc640fdae91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 06:34:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] The gatefold, the credits, the shop window, and what a
 record measures
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

R6, R7, R9, R10, R11, T2, T3 — the last of the approved list.

A double album opens. Thickness already followed track count, a
multi-disc release already drew as more than one sleeve, and the back
already turned; a gatefold is the obvious next member of that family and
the most characteristic object in the whole subject. The inner spread is
two panels on one hinge with the tracklist running across both, broken
at the disc, which is how a real one sets it. It replaces the plain back
rather than joining it — the back of a gatefold sleeve is its own outer
panel, and that is what the single-sleeve back already is. Where there
is no room to open sideways, the spread stacks.

The credits are on the stage. The tag reader pulls composer, lyricist,
conductor, publisher, copyright and ISRC out of every container it
handles, and the parser threw all of it away before the track was
written — a whole panel of real information parsed and dropped on the
floor. It reads the file's own fields and prints nothing where there is
nothing, so the button hides itself rather than opening an empty panel.

A cover can be looked at. Artwork is imported at about 448px and drawn
at 232, and clicking one navigated, so there was no way to simply see
the picture. The lightbox shows the largest thing Sonora holds and says
how big that actually is — a 300px cover shown full-screen should admit
to being a 300px cover.

Wear, off by default. This is the one piece of pure decoration here that
earns its place: the sleeve model already lights and shades itself from
the artwork's own luminance and playCount has been kept since the first
release, so a record played four hundred times looking played is the
same fact the library already holds, said in the material rather than in
a number. Logarithmic — the difference between never and twice is the
interesting one — and bounded so a favourite is always still readable.

A shop window. There is a full 3D world here and after a few minutes of
nobody touching anything it sat there, while a player left running on a
second screen is one of the main ways people use one. Any input cancels
it, including the one that would have started it; it never runs while an
import does; and with reduced motion asked for, the covers cut rather
than glide.

And what a record measures, on the record's own page: dynamic range per
track and averaged, tempo where the analysis was confident, brightness,
and the encoder shelf that catches a lossless container made from a
lossy source. No new analysis — every figure is already on the track —
and every one says how much of the record it has seen, because a figure
is only known for a track that has been listened to and a bare "DR11"
over nine tracks would be a claim about five of them nobody has made.

The README is brought up to date, including its own account of what this
